<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuhui Xu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Salesforce AI Research
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Christian Herold
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>eBay Inc.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anders Søgaard
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Copenhagen University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Maarten de Rijke
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>eBay Inc.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Christof Monz
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Amsterdam
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have significantly advanced complex reasoning capabilities, particularly through extended chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning that incorporates mechanisms such as backtracking, self-reflection, and self-correction. Despite these developments, the self-correction abilities of LLMs during long CoT reasoning remain underexplored. And recent findings on overthinking suggest that such models often engage in unnecessarily redundant reasoning. In this work, we empirically show that the first reasoning step exerts a disproportionately large influence on the final prediction—errors introduced at this stage can substantially degrade subsequent reasoning quality. This phenomenon is consistently observed across two state-of-the-art open-source reasoning model families: DeepSeek-R1 and Qwen3. To address this, we propose an efficient sampling strategy that leverages a reward model to identify and retain high-quality first reasoning steps while discarding suboptimal ones, achieving up to a 70% reduction in inference cost without sacrificing accuracy. Finally, we introduce a new benchmark specifically constructed with deliberately flawed first reasoning steps to systematically evaluate models’ self-correction capabilities, offering a foundation for future research on robust reasoning in LLMs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">万事开头难。 (The first step is always difficult.)</em></p>
<p id="p1.1.2" class="ltx_p">— Chinese Proverb</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable performance across a variety of reasoning tasks, ranging from mathematical problem solving to multi-hop question answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. More recently, the advent of reasoning-oriented LLMs capable of performing long chain-of-thought (long-CoT) reasoning at test time has led to substantial advancements in these domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. A widely held hypothesis attributes this progress to the models’ ability to backtrack, self-reflect, and self-correct, effectively leveraging contextual feedback to iteratively refine their responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have suggested that LLMs engaged in long-CoT reasoning may exhibit a tendency to overthink—that is, to produce unnecessarily extended reasoning processes to solve problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. This observation raises questions about the model’s capacity for backtracking, self-reflection, and self-correction. It suggests that LLMs may lack awareness of the information they have already processed, leading to redundant or inefficient reasoning. Moreover, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. [17]</a></cite> demonstrates that LLMs are prone to the "lost-in-the-middle" phenomenon, wherein information located in the middle of a long context is often overlooked. While their analysis is conducted in the context of information retrieval, it remains an open question whether similar positional biases affect long CoT as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce a novel and previously underexplored perspective on long-CoT reasoning: many reasoning failures in long-CoT LLMs stem not from errors made mid-chain, but rather from flaws at the beginning of reasoning. Our experiments demonstrate that the first reasoning step has the most significant influence on the final prediction. When this first step is incorrect, the model is considerably more likely to arrive at an incorrect final answer (40% accuracy drop), highlighting the limited self-correction capabilities of current long-CoT LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by this finding, we propose an efficient early pruning algorithm that exploits the predictive power of the first reasoning step. Specifically, by evaluating the quality of the first step, we identify and discard less promising reasoning paths early, continuing generation only for the more promising ones. This approach significantly reduces inference cost. Across four long-CoT LLMs and four challenging mathematical and scientific reasoning benchmarks, our method maintains prediction accuracy while reducing inference budget by up to 70%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we introduce <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LaBoR</span>, a self-correction benchmark constructed by an automatic pipeline, aiming at quantifying LLMs’ ability to recover from initial reasoning errors. Each LaBoR instance contains a question paired with a faulty first reasoning step, requiring the model to detect and correct the initial error to produce the correct final answer. To our knowledge, LaBoR is the first benchmark specifically designed to assess self-correction in long-CoT settings. Despite its simplicity, LaBoR effectively captures the extent to which LLMs struggle to recover from early mistakes. Through extensive evaluation of eight state-of-the-art open-source long-CoT LLMs, we observe a substantial vulnerability: performance drops by up to 11.4% when the initial step contains an error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> Our main contributions are as follows:
(1) We empirically establish a strong positive correlation between the first reasoning step and the final prediction (§<a href="#S3" title="3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>);
(2) We propose an efficient early pruning algorithm that halts generation for less promising initial steps, thereby improving inference efficiency (§<a href="#S4" title="4 Early pruning with hint from first step ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>);
(3) We introduce <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LaBoR</span>, a simple and automatic benchmark construction pipeline to facilitate future research on the self-correction capabilities of long-CoT LLMs (§<a href="#S5" title="5 LaBoR: self-correction benchmark ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lost in the middle.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. [17]</a></cite> introduced the "lost in the middle" effect, demonstrating that LLMs tend to overlook information in the middle of long contexts, performing better when relevant content appears at the beginning or end. This positional bias is evident across tasks like arithmetic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, multiple-choice QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, text evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, passage ranking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, and instructional prompt positioning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Additionally, primacy and recency biases, where attention skews toward initial and final tokens irrespective of semantic relevance, have also been reported <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. While previous studies primarily examined positional biases in external context processing, we investigate whether similar biases affect internal reasoning trajectories in long CoT models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-correction.</span> Self-correction in LLMs refers to their capability to revise reasoning to enhance final answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. Recent models trained via reinforcement learning exhibit emergent self-reflection abilities, revisiting and adjusting prior reasoning steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Existing research typically evaluates self-correction by instructing models explicitly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> or via external feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. In contrast, we explore self-correction as an intrinsic reasoning capability, investigating models’ ability to independently detect and recover from flawed initial reasoning steps without external signals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Lost at the beginning of reasoning</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the finding of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. [17]</a></cite>, which demonstrates that query-relevant information is more impactful when positioned at either the beginning or end of an LLM’s context window, we first investigate whether a similar positional effect exists in long-CoT reasoning (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Semantic similarity between different reasoning steps and the final conclusion ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). Our analysis reveals that the first reasoning step has the greatest impact to the final conclusion. To validate this observation, we further perform two ablation studies, confirming the critical role of the first step in determining the model’s final prediction (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Correlation between the first reasoning step and the final prediction ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and §<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Minor perturbation to the correct first step leads to significant loss ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation.</span> Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> represent the input prompt, consisting of both a system instruction and a user query. A reasoning model <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a sequence of CoT reasoning steps <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=[t_{1},t_{2},...,t_{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=[t_{1},t_{2},...,t_{T}]</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by a final conclusion <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the complete model output is given by <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\oplus c_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\oplus c_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> means concatenation. In models such as DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, the input-output format adheres to the following:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p\ \mathrm{{&lt;}think{&gt;}}\ t_{1},t_{2},\ldots,t_{T}\ \mathrm{{&lt;}/think{&gt;}}\ c_{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.778em">&lt;</mo><mi>think</mi><mo rspace="0.778em">&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mi>think</mi><mo rspace="0.778em">&gt;</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\ \mathrm{{&lt;}think{&gt;}}\ t_{1},t_{2},\ldots,t_{T}\ \mathrm{{&lt;}/think{&gt;}}\ c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The final prediction <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is then derived by applying an extraction function <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> to the conclusion, i.e., <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}=g(c_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}=g(c_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> may, for example, extract values enclosed within <span id="S3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{}</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The conclusion <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as a summary of the essential reasoning steps leading to the final prediction. This raises an interesting question: <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is there a positional bias in how reasoning steps contribute to the conclusion?</span> In other words, do certain steps have a disproportionately greater influence on <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> than others?</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Semantic similarity between different reasoning steps and the final conclusion</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand how different reasoning steps contribute to the final conclusion, we measure the semantic similarity between each reasoning step <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and the final conclusion <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental setup.</span>
We evaluate a total of 60 questions from AIME24 and AIME25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, sampling 64 CoT traces per question using <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B</span> (abbreviated as <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DS-R1-Qwen-7B</span> in the remainder of this paper) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Generation is performed with <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">temperature=1.0</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">top_p=0.9</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">min_p=0.05</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_tokens=32768</span>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            All subsequent experiments use the same decoding hyperparameters.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure" style="width:47%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22058v1/semantic_similarity_plot_longest.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/157;" width="224" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cosine similarity between the embeddings of the i-th reasoning step <math id="S3.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the final conclusion <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the maximum number of reasoning steps for interpolation. See Figure <a href="#A4.F1" title="Figure D.1 ‣ Appendix D Semantic similarity between first reasoning step and final answer ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> for results based on the average number of reasoning steps used for interpolation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following the setup in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. [39]</a></cite>, we define a reasoning step as the text span beginning with the token “Alternatively”—that is, the first token in step <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{i}\}_{i=2}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{i}\}_{i=2}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is ‘‘Alternatively’’.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            We empirically found ”Alternatively” to be an effective delimiter for segmenting reasoning steps, as it commonly appears in CoT traces and often marks a shift in reasoning. In contrast, “Wait” is overly frequent and ambiguous, while “Let me verify” is too rare.</span></span></span> To measure semantic alignment between the reasoning steps and final conclusion, we compute the cosine similarity between their embeddings using <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span> from the SentenceTransformers library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. Since different CoT traces contain varying numbers of reasoning steps, we interpolate the similarity scores of different traces to a fixed length (maximum number of reasoning steps or average number of reasoning steps over all traces) for a better visualization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result.</span> From Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Semantic similarity between different reasoning steps and the final conclusion ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we notice that the first reasoning step exhibits the highest semantic similarity to the final conclusion. This similarity gradually declines across subsequent steps. These observations suggest that the first step, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, plays a pivotal role in establishing the semantic trajectory of the reasoning process and is most closely aligned with the eventual outcome. The continued decrease in similarity implies that the model explores intermediate reasoning paths that diverge from the final conclusion before ultimately re-converging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the strong alignment between early reasoning steps—particularly the first—and the final conclusion, we hypothesize that the first step may significantly influence whether the reasoning model can arrive at a correct prediction.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22058v1/first_reason_accuracy_deepseek-r1.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/298;" width="476" height="298"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22058v1/first_reason_number_of_tokens_deepseek-r1.svg" id="S3.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/298;" width="476" height="298"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy and number of tokens on DS-R1-Qwen-7B. <span id="S3.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> The relationship between the accuracy of the final prediction (<math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the correctness of the prediction solely based on the first reasoning step (<math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) across different difficulty levels. If <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is incorrect, <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is more likely incorrect. <span id="S3.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> The number of tokens used for the final prediction after the first reasoning step <math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the number of tokens used for <math id="S3.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{2},t_{3},...,t_{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{2},t_{3},...,t_{T}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Although <math id="S3.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is correct, the model still consumes a large amount of tokens for the following reasoning steps–overthinking.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Correlation between the first reasoning step and the final prediction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given that the first reasoning step closely resembles the final conclusion, we investigate whether the essential reasoning required for the final prediction is already encapsulated in the first step. To this end, we analyze the prediction when conditioned solely on the first reasoning step. Specifically, we compute <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{1}=\mathcal{M}(p\mathrm{{&lt;}think{&gt;}}t_{1}\mathrm{{&lt;}/think{&gt;}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>think</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mi>think</mi><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}=\mathcal{M}(p\mathrm{{&lt;}think{&gt;}}t_{1}\mathrm{{&lt;}/think{&gt;}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and derive the corresponding prediction <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}=g(c_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}=g(c_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which we compare against the ground truth <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on this comparison, we categorize each first reasoning step as either <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">first correct</span> (if <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}=a</annotation></semantics></math>) or <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">first incorrect</span> (otherwise).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental setup.</span> From the questions and their CoT traces obtained in §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Semantic similarity between different reasoning steps and the final conclusion ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we exclude questions for which all 64 CoT traces result in either correct or incorrect predictions, as these are considered either too easy or too difficult, respectively, yielding 38 questions for DS-R1-Qwen-7B and 37 for Qwen3-8B. For each remaining question and its corresponding first reasoning step <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the initial prediction <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as previously described. To better visualize the final outcomes, we categorize the questions into three groups based on the pass@1 accuracy of the final prediction <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>,<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            That is, the average accuracy across 64 CoT traces.</span></span></span> corresponding to the intervals (0, 33%], (33%, 67%], and (67%, 100%). A higher pass@1 indicates a simpler question. This grouping allows us to assess whether our observations hold consistently across varying levels of question difficulty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result.</span> As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Semantic similarity between different reasoning steps and the final conclusion ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Left), the commonly assumed self-correction capability of reasoning models appears to be overstated. When the first reasoning step is incorrect, the model’s final prediction is also likely to be incorrect. On average, final prediction accuracy drops by 40% when the first reasoning step is incorrect, with the most substantial decrease (43%) occurring for difficult questions (0–33% range) and a notable decline (33%) even for easier questions (67–100% range). In addition, we also compute the Pearson correlation between the correctness of the first prediction <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the final prediction <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> over all questions. The coefficient <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.60</annotation></semantics></math> and p-value <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0</annotation></semantics></math> denote a moderately strong positive correlation. All these results underscore the pivotal role of the first reasoning step in steering the model toward a correct final answer, particularly in more complex instances where recovery from early mistakes is more challenging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Semantic similarity between different reasoning steps and the final conclusion ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Right) further illustrates the model’s tendency toward overthinking. Even when the first reasoning step is sufficient to arrive at the correct answer, the model continues to generate a substantial number of additional reasoning tokens—the same scale in length to those generated following an incorrect first step. Both findings are also observed for Qwen3-8B in Figure <a href="#A8.F1" title="Figure H.1 ‣ Appendix H Correlation between first reasoning step and final prediction in Qwen3 ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.1</span></a>, reinforcing this pattern across models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Minor perturbation to the correct first step leads to significant loss</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on our findings in Section §<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Correlation between the first reasoning step and the final prediction ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, which demonstrate a positive correlation between the model’s first and final predictions, we further investigate the significance of the first reasoning step by introducing minor perturbations. Specifically, we slightly alter an initially correct reasoning step and provide it as input to the model to assess whether it can recover from such errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental setup.</span> Unlike Section §<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Correlation between the first reasoning step and the final prediction ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, where we analyze the correctness of the first reasoning step <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, here we treat the final correct conclusion <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>—which satisfies <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}=g(c_{T})=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}=g(c_{T})=a</annotation></semantics></math>—as the new first reasoning step, denoted <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This choice ensures that the step contains all necessary reasoning for arriving at the correct answer, which cannot be guaranteed for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Semantic similarity between different reasoning steps and the final conclusion ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Left), an initially correct reasoning step can still lead to an incorrect final prediction. To construct <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply the following perturbations to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Examples for minor perturbation ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for an example): (1) we remove not only the explicit answer formatting (e.g., <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{a}</span>) but also any surrounding sentences that may directly disclose or repeat the final answer; (2) the resulting text from (1) is treated as the correct version of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (serving as our baseline); (3) we generate an incorrect version by replacing the correct answer <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in the remaining reasoning with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\pm 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\pm 10</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            The answer of AIME question is integer in the range of <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[0,999)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,999</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,999)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table" style="width:68%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Perturbation experiments. Accuracy refers to the correctness of the final prediction; #Tokens indicates average number of generated tokens per question. A minor perturbation leads to a significant accuracy drop.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Model</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Correct first step</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Incorrect first step</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Acc.<span id="S3.T1.5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (%)</span></span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">#Tokens</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Acc.<span id="S3.T1.5.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (%)</span></span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">#Tokens</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">DS-R1-Qwen-7B</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">94.8</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">945.5</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.3.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">28.5</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.3.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1119.9</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qwen3-8B</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">71.4</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">7930.0</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">37.0</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.3pt;padding-right:4.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.5.4.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">8087.1</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These perturbations are minimal, as they preserve the core reasoning structure while only altering the final prediction in the incorrect variant. We then combine the prompt <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with the modified first reasoning step <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and input it to the model as <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(p\mathrm{{&lt;}think{&gt;}}t^{\prime}_{1}\mathrm{Alternatively})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>think</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Alternatively</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(p\mathrm{{&lt;}think{&gt;}}t^{\prime}_{1}\mathrm{Alternatively})</annotation></semantics></math> to assess subsequent reasoning behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Minor perturbation to the correct first step leads to significant loss ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we make two key observations: (1) Even when the first reasoning step is correct, the model does not achieve 100% accuracy, suggesting that it may experience uncertainty or revise its initial judgment, as indicated by the use of terms like “Alternatively”; (2) There is a substantial drop in accuracy when the first reasoning step is incorrect, highlighting that even minor errors early in the reasoning process can significantly affect the final prediction. These findings further indicate that the LLM’s ability to self-correct has been overestimated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we observe that the reasoning model is particularly vulnerable at the initial stage of the reasoning process; an error in the first step can propagate and substantially degrade the final prediction. <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we develop a method to identify and retain more promising first reasoning steps while discarding suboptimal ones to enhance the overall generation efficiency?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Early pruning with hint from first step</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose an efficient and straightforward sampling method to identify a promising first reasoning step. By doing so, we can terminate the generation process early when a suboptimal first step is detected, thereby reducing unnecessary computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Problem definition</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the notation introduced in Section §<a href="#S3" title="3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we incorporate a random seed <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to introduce stochasticity into the sampling process. Specifically, a sampled trace is computed as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\oplus c_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\oplus c_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. By varying the random seed <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain diverse generations, denoted as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}\oplus c^{n}_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p,\epsilon^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}\oplus c^{n}_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p,\epsilon^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}=[t^{n}_{1},t^{n}_{2},\ldots,t^{n}_{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}=[t^{n}_{1},t^{n}_{2},\ldots,t^{n}_{T}]</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            In prior experiments, we sampled 64 CoT traces per question using 64 distinct random seeds <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\epsilon^{n}\}_{n=1}^{64}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>64</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\epsilon^{n}\}_{n=1}^{64}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A widely adopted technique for reasoning tasks is majority voting or self-consistency generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. To promote exploration of the reasoning space, models are typically sampled at high temperatures, resulting in diverse outputs. Majority voting then serves to aggregate these outputs by selecting the most frequent final prediction. Formally, majority voting over <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> samples is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{maj@}K}=\text{mode}(\{q^{n}_{T}\}_{n=1}^{K})\quad\text{where}\quad q^{n}_{T}=g(c^{n}_{T})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mtext>maj@</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>mode</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{maj@}K}=\text{mode}(\{q^{n}_{T}\}_{n=1}^{K})\quad\text{where}\quad q^{n}_{T}=g(c^{n}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, for models generating long CoT traces, majority voting becomes computationally expensive, as it requires sampling <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> complete reasoning paths independently. In this section, we propose a more efficient approach that samples only <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> full traces, where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>, while maintaining comparable majority voting performance to the one with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samplings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Methodology</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section §<a href="#S3" title="3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we demonstrated a strong positive correlation between the first reasoning step and the final prediction. This observation motivates a method that identifies the top <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> most promising first reasoning steps out of a total of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and continues generation only for these selected <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> candidates, while discarding the remaining <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let a reasoning model generate <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate first reasoning step <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{1}^{1},t_{1}^{2},\dots,t_{1}^{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{1}^{1},t_{1}^{2},\dots,t_{1}^{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from a prompt <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with different random seeds <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\epsilon^{n}\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\epsilon^{n}\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first reasoning step of a full reasoning trajectory. We define a scoring function <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:t_{1}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:t_{1}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> that estimates the promise of a first step, e.g., rating from a reward model. We then select the top <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> first steps based on their scores:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{top}}=\text{TopM}(\{r(t_{1}^{n})\}_{n=1}^{N})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>top</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>TopM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{top}}=\text{TopM}(\{r(t_{1}^{n})\}_{n=1}^{N})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Only the selected <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> first steps <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{1}^{n}\mid n\in\mathcal{R}_{\text{top}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>top</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{1}^{n}\mid n\in\mathcal{R}_{\text{top}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are used for further multi-step generation. The remaining <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-M)</annotation></semantics></math> are discarded. Since the first step typically requires only a small number of tokens to generate, this selection approach efficiently reduces computation by avoiding full sampling for the less promising <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-M)</annotation></semantics></math> candidates.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22058v1/maj.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/252;" width="467" height="252"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Majority voting with different number of samplings on DS-R1-Qwen-1.5B (1st row), 8B (2nd row), Qwen3-8B (3rd row) and Skywork-OR1-7B-Preview (4th row). The vertical dashed line denotes the smallest <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> whose accuracy is equal to or larger than the accuracy of <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Experiments</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> All inference experiments are conducted on NVIDIA A100-80GB GPU, using the vLLM framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. We evaluate four advanced reasoning models—<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Skywork-OR1-7B-Preview</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>—across four challenging reasoning benchmarks: AIME24, AIME25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, HMMT Feb 2025 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, and the GPQA Diamond subset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, consisting of 30, 30, 30, and 198 questions respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider values of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> from the set <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,4,8,16,32,64\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,4,8,16,32,64\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For early pruning, we fix <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math> and select the top <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> most promising first-step candidates using reward scores predicted by a process reward model (PRM), <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. When <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=64</annotation></semantics></math>, the accuracy is exactly the same as the one for <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math>, since all candidates are chosen. For PRM, a “step” is defined as a segment of text ending with “<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\n\n</span>”, and each step receives an individual reward score. The trajectory <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains multiple such steps, and we use the score of its final step as the overall reward, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(t^{n}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(t^{n}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, using a PRM to score <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is really cheap, because <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is short, and its computation is similar to generate one token with input <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\oplus t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\oplus t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike the definition used in Section §<a href="#S3" title="3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> terminates upon generating the keyword “Alternatively”, we redefine <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in this section to have a fixed token length, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The previous definition made it difficult to control the computational budget for generating first steps, as the trigger phrase might not appear or could occur late in the sequence. By fixing the token length, we achieve precise control over the generation budget when sampling <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate first steps. By default, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})=512</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result.</span> As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Methodology ‣ 4 Early pruning with hint from first step ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, selecting the top 16 first reasoning steps from a pool of 64 candidates and continuing generation from these yields performance that matches or even surpasses that of conventional sampling with <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math>. This pattern holds consistently across various reasoning models and benchmarks. Notably, for certain benchmarks and models—such as HMMT on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B, as well as AIME24, AIME25, and GPQA on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B—setting <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficient to achieve performance comparable to using all 64 samples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Early pruning accuracy and efficiency. We select <math id="S4.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=16</annotation></semantics></math> first steps with the highest reward scores out of <math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math> candidate first steps. The number of tokens used for the 48 discarded first steps is also included for the early pruning method. Early pruning maintains the accuracy, even improves sometimes, while only requiring <math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;30\%</annotation></semantics></math> original inference budget.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;">Model</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="5"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;">Maj@K accuracy (%)<span id="S4.T2.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></td>
