<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22058v1/samping_ablation.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/129;" width="405" height="129"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation studies on DS-R1-Qwen-32B, with average accuracy across AIME24, AIME25 and HMMT. <span id="S4.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Comparison of reward signals derived from different reward models. The choice of reward model has minimal impact on overall performance. <span id="S4.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Effect of varying the length of the first reasoning step. Using a very short first step, like 256 tokens, leads to suboptimal performance, likely because it provides insufficient reasoning context to effectively evaluate the quality of the step.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Experiments ‣ 4 Early pruning with hint from first step ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the detailed token counts associated with different methods. With <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=16</annotation></semantics></math>, early pruning consistently preserves the accuracy across various models and benchmarks. Remarkably, for Qwen3-8B, early pruning even improves the average accuracy by 1.1%. Despite this performance gain, it utilizes only <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;30\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the inference budget required by standard sampling for majority voting with <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighting the substantial efficiency benefits of our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation.</span> We further conduct additional ablation studies to validate our default settings. In Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Experiments ‣ 4 Early pruning with hint from first step ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (Left), we evaluate two additional reward models: a larger PRM, Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-72B, and an outcome reward model, Qwen2.5-Math-RM-72B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. The results indicate that the choice of reward model has minimal impact on performance. Notably, the smaller 7B PRM achieves comparable results, highlighting the efficiency of our approach. In Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Experiments ‣ 4 Early pruning with hint from first step ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (Right), we examine the impact of varying the length of the first reasoning step. We observe that the shortest first step (i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})=256</annotation></semantics></math>) leads to degraded performance. We hypothesize that shorter <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> sequences lack sufficient reasoning content, making them less informative for reliable reward model evaluation. Nevertheless, setting <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})\geq 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})\geq 512</annotation></semantics></math> tokens yields consistently better performance than vanilla sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion.</span> With the emergence of long CoT models such as DeepSeek-R1, PRMs have received less attention for two main reasons: (1) PRM training is less scalable due to the high cost of acquiring step-wise annotated data, limiting their applicability to specific domains; and (2) most open-source PRMs are constrained by short context windows (e.g., 4K tokens), which are incompatible with the extended reasoning capabilities of long CoT models (e.g., 32K tokens). In this work, we demonstrate a promising approach that leverages short-context PRMs (or RMs) to guide long CoT models toward more efficient generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>LaBoR: self-correction benchmark</h2>

<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22058v1/benchmark_results.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/164;" width="467" height="164"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance on LaBoR. <span id="S5.F5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Accuracy of the final prediction (<math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) depending on whether the first reasoning step is included as input. The performance difference is marked in red. <span id="S5.F5.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> The number of tokens used for the final prediction.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we observe that reasoning models exhibit a pronounced vulnerability to initial errors, highlighting their limited capacity for self-correction. Despite the importance of this limitation, there currently exists no benchmark specifically designed to quantitatively assess the self-correction ability of long-CoT LLMs. To address this gap, we propose <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LaBoR</span> (<span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">L</span>ost <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">a</span>t the <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">B</span>eginning <span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">o</span>f <span id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">R</span>easoning), an automatically constructed benchmark aimed at facilitating systematic evaluation and advancing research in this area.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Benchmark construction</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct our self-correction benchmark, LaBoR, with the following automatic pipeline:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I. Question collection.</span> We compile a total of 100 questions sourced from AIME24, AIME25, and HMMT, along with 10 reference questions from AIMO2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. We exclude GPQA due to its multiple-choice format, which allows models to potentially guess correct answers. Additionally, we prioritize recent questions to reduce the risk of data leakage from training corpora.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II. First step generation.</span> For each question, we use DS-R1-Qwen-7B to generate up to 8 candidate first reasoning steps <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t^{n}_{1}\}_{n=1}^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>8</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t^{n}_{1}\}_{n=1}^{8}</annotation></semantics></math>, terminating generation when the trigger phrase “Alternatively” is produced. This phrase indicates a shift in reasoning, which we use to identify plausible initial errors. We discard reasoning steps that do not contain this phrase, as they likely represent coherent and correct thought processes. This results in a pool of 667 candidate first steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">III. First step filtering.</span> We then prompt DS-R1-Qwen-7B to predict an answer <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on the question and each individual first step, as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}(p&lt;\text{think}&gt;t_{1}^{n}&lt;/\text{think}&gt;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mtext>think</mtext><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mtext>think</mtext><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(p&lt;\text{think}&gt;t_{1}^{n}&lt;/\text{think}&gt;)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is correct, we infer that the reasoning step likely contains necessary reasoning for a correct prediction, and exclude it. After this filtering step, 495 first reasoning steps remain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IV. Cross-model verification.</span> To further refine the dataset, we feed each remaining first step into both DS-R1-Qwen-7B and DS-R1-Qwen-14B using <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(p&lt;\text{think}&gt;t_{1}^{n}\text{ Alternatively})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mtext>think</mtext><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> Alternatively</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(p&lt;\text{think}&gt;t_{1}^{n}\text{ Alternatively})</annotation></semantics></math> and collect the final prediction <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. If either model produces a correct answer, we remove the corresponding first step, as this suggests the incorrect reasoning is too easily recoverable. This step yields 301 surviving reasoning steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V. Per-question sampling.</span> To reduce the cost of model evaluation, we randomly retain one first reasoning step per question. This results in a final set of 53 questions, each paired with a corresponding incorrect first reasoning step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Descriptive statistics.</span> Among the 53 questions in LaBoR, 8 are sourced from AIME24, 17 from AIME25, 4 from AIMO2, and 24 from HMMT. The number of tokens in the first reasoning steps ranges from 344 to 3928, with an average of 1355 tokens. Two data points are illustrated in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F LaBoR benchmark examples ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, along with the corresponding human-annotated error labels.<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            The errors are only annotated for these two samples.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We construct LaBoR automatically for two key reasons. First, the questions involved are sourced from mathematics competitions and typically require substantial domain expertise to solve. Manually annotating the first incorrect reasoning step would necessitate hiring mathematics experts, making the process both costly and unsustainable. Second, given the rapid advancements in LLMs, we anticipate that our benchmark will become solvable in the near future, while new, challenging questions will continue to emerge. An automated construction pipeline enables us to efficiently regenerate updated versions of LaBoR as needed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LaBoR is designed as a self-correction benchmark, which requires the reasoning model to interpret the first incorrect reasoning step as if it were generated by the model itself. To evaluate this behavior, we prepend the first erroneous step to the model’s reasoning process using the format <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(p&lt;\text{think}&gt;t_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mtext>think</mtext><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(p&lt;\text{think}&gt;t_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, prompting the model to continue reasoning from that point. Importantly, if this first step is instead included alongside the question in the user prompt <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> before the thinking tag, the model may misinterpret it as a distractor or part of the input query—an unintended behavior. Unfortunately, the desired format is only supported by self-deployed models. The APIs of closed-source models generally require both the question and the first step to be provided together in the user message, making it impossible to put the first reasoning step inside the thinking tags. As a result, our evaluations primarily focus on open-source reasoning models, specifically from the DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, Skywork <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and Light-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> families.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5 LaBoR: self-correction benchmark ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents the performance of various models on the LaBoR benchmark. We observe the following key findings: (1) The filtered questions in LaBoR are highly challenging, with the best-performing model, Qwen3-235B-A22B, achieving only 47.2% accuracy. This suggests that LaBoR can also serve as a pure math reasoning benchmark. (2) Most models experience a substantial drop in accuracy when an incorrect first reasoning step is provided, compared to the question-only setting—typically ranging from a 3.7% to 11.4% accuracy decline. The only exception is Qwen3-235B-A22B, which remains unaffected by the incorrect first reasoning step. (3) Both Skywork-OR1-32B and Light-R1-32B-DS are finetuned from DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B. While they significantly outperform the base model in the question-only setting, they suffer from greater degradation when presented with an incorrect first step (9.4% and 9.5% vs. 3.7% drop). (4) The number of tokens used in the question-only setting is consistently lower than in the setting that includes both the question and the incorrect first step, indicating that recovering from an error requires more extensive reasoning and thus higher token consumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we report evaluation results on several closed models, including OpenAI o4-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, Gemini-2.5-Flash-Preview <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, and Claude-3.7-Sonnet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, in Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Closed-source model performance on the LaBoR benchmark ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>. Due to format limitations—specifically, the inability to insert the first reasoning step within the appropriate thinking tags—these results deviate from the main observations. We strongly recommend using our prescribed input format to accurately assess a model’s ability to self-correct.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we empirically demonstrate that the first reasoning step plays a critical role in determining the final outcome of a model’s reasoning process. Errors introduced early can significantly degrade overall performance. Motivated by this observation, we propose an efficient sampling strategy that identifies and retains high-quality first reasoning steps, reducing the inference computation up to 70% across three model families. To better understand models’ resilience to early errors, we introduce a benchmark featuring intentionally flawed first steps, enabling systematic assessment of self-correction capabilities of reasoning models. We hope this benchmark supports future research toward more robust and reliable reasoning in LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
